The rule for conformance.kernel.recovery states: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text" [65ae18af7a738b26]. However, fence content must parse as JSON to be scored directly from the fence; if it contains multiple objects (fence[ A B ]), recovery falls back to top-level object scanning [cd36f9e459490eb4]. In the input constructed from cell fence[ A B ] C, the fence contains objects A and B on separate lines, which does not parse as a single JSON object. The rule falls to the else clause: "the last top-level object in the text", scanning all objects including those within unparseable fence content. C is the last top-level object, so C is scored. In the rewritten text, object C following the fence is removed, leaving only fence[ A B ]. The fence still contains two unparseable objects, so recovery falls back to top-level scanning where B is now the last object. Therefore, the recovered object moves from C to B.